+### Gaussian Parameter Prediction
+Gaussian Parameter Prediction unprojects per-pixel depth maps to 3D space using camera parameters to obtain Gaussian centers $\mu_j$ [S5]. It concatenates the original image, depth map, and monocular feature information, then passes this through an additional DPT head to predict remaining Gaussian parameters: opacity $\alpha_j$, covariance $\Sigma_j$, and color $c_j$ [S5]. Novel views are rendered using the predicted 3D Gaussians with the Gaussian splatting operation [S5].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method operates in a feed-forward manner, avoiding the slow, per-scene optimization required by standard 3DGS [S2]. The use of a sparse 3D CNN and downsampling of initial primitives reduces computation compared to pixel-aligned methods [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The global volumetric representation requires memory for the feature volume $F$ and the point cloud $P$. While it reduces redundancy compared to per-pixel methods, it may have a larger footprint than local volume methods due to the global nature [S2].
-*   **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering by feed-forward prediction of Gaussian primitives [S2]. It is designed for urban scene reconstruction from sparse input views [S2].
-*   **Scaling:** The method scales to urban scenes with large camera movements and small parallax, where methods requiring image overlaps struggle [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves global consistency, reduces ghosting artifacts, handles foreground/background separation, and enables fast feed-forward reconstruction [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of off-the-shelf depth estimators; noisy depth predictions require filtering [S2].
-*   **When to use:** Efficient urban view synthesis from sparse views where real-time rendering and global consistency are critical [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** Small-scale scenes with high overlap where per-scene optimization might yield higher fidelity, or scenes where depth estimation is unreliable [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward inference is significantly faster than per-scene optimization methods (like standard 3DGS), which require minutes of gradient descent. The method involves a single pass through a 3D CNN and MLP heads, plus recursive offset updates (typically 2 iterations for convergence) [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing a global feature volume $F$ and the point cloud $P$. The sparse 3D CNN reduces memory usage compared to dense volumes, but it is still higher than pixel-aligned methods that do not maintain a global 3D structure [S2].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves real-time rendering speeds due to the feed-forward nature and efficient rasterization of 3D Gaussians [S2]. It improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to existing feed-forward methods that predict per-pixel aligned Gaussians using local cost volumes or epipolar transformers [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles large-scale urban scenes with large camera movements and small parallax, where traditional multi-view stereo methods fail [S2]. Effectively removes floating artifacts and redundant primitives [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of off-the-shelf monocular depth estimators; noisy depth can lead to initial inaccuracies, though the recursive refinement helps [S2]. Requires calibrated camera poses for unprojection [S2].
+*   **When to Use:** Suitable for urban view synthesis, autonomous driving applications, and large-scale scene reconstruction where global consistency is critical and per-scene optimization is too slow [S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for small-scale scenes with high overlap where local cost volumes might be more efficient, or when camera poses are unknown/unavailable [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction, specifically in the form of EVolSplat, was introduced by Sheng Miao, Jiaxin Huang, Dongfeng Bai, Xu Yan, Hongyu Zhou, Yue Wang, Bingbing Liu, Andreas Geiger, and Yiyi Liao from Zhejiang University, Huawei Noah’s Ark Lab, and the University of Tübingen/Tübingen AI Center [S2]. It was published in CVPR 2025 [S2]. The method addresses limitations in existing feed-forward 3DGS methods like MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward, which suffer from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content due to local volume accumulation [S2]. It builds on the foundation of 3D Gaussian Splatting [15] and integrates depth priors from metric depth estimators [S2].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction, specifically in the form of EVolSplat, was introduced by Sheng Miao, Jiaxin Huang, Dongfeng Bai, Xu Yan, Hongyu Zhou, Yue Wang, Bingbing Liu, Andreas Geiger, and Yiyi Liao from Zhejiang University, Huawei Noah’s Ark Lab, and the University of Tübingen [S2]. It was published in CVPR 2025 [S2]. The method addresses the limitations of existing feed-forward 3DGS methods, such as MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward, which suffer from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content when accumulating local predictions [S2]. It builds on the foundation of 3D Gaussian Splatting [15] introduced by Kerbl et al., which explicitly parameterizes the 3D radiance field as a collection of 3D Gaussians for efficient rendering [S2].
 
